db/ref 17592186516214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516750 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7a92c6e-2892-5988-ae79-425c23feccca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516780 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516780 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516780 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516780 #edb/ref 1071 "A player's height in a particular professional basketball league is normally distributed with a mean of $77$ inches and a standard deviation of $3.57$ inches.\nWhat is the probability that the height of a randomly selected player is more than $76$ inches but less than $78$ inches?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5636\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5675\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5714\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6026\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6064\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6103\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6406\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6443\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6480\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4364\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516780 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a1059d5-573e-59f8-8d92-01104e325d82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516780 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the height (in inches) of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(77,{3.57}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(76<X<78).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(76<X<78) & =P(\\frac{76-77}{3.57}<Z<\\frac{78-77}{3.57}) \\\\  & =P(-0.28<Z<0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.28<Z<0.28) & =P(Z<0.28)-P(Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.28)-P(Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =Φ(0.28)-Φ(-0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.28) & =0.6103 \\\\ Φ(-0.28) & =0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.28<Z<0.28) & =Φ(0.28)-Φ(-0.28) \\\\  & =0.6103-0.3897 \\\\  & =0.2206 \\\\  & ≈0.22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the height of a randomly selected player is more than $76$ inches but less than $78$ inches is approximately $0.22.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516814 #edb/ref 1071 "The average volume of soda in cans is $12$ ounces. Additionally, in $3%$ of cans, the soda volume is less than $11.7$ ounces. Assuming the volume of soda is normally distributed, find the percentage of cans containing less than $12.2$ ounces of soda, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7012a871-ceb2-5bf5-9d51-cddf37c78344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516814 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the volume of soda in a randomly selected can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12.2).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<11.7)=0.03.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11.7) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11.7-12}{\\sigma }) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma }) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)≈-1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & =-1.8808 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =6.26933 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{6.26933} \\\\  & ≈0.1595.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12.2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12.2) & =P(X\\le 12.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12.2-12}{0.1595}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25) \\\\  & ≈89.4%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $89.4%$  of soda cans contain less than $12.2$ ounces of soda." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516843 #edb/ref 1071 "The annual income of an employee in a large company is normally distributed with a mean of $$50\\,000$ and a standard deviation of $$15\\,000.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected employee's income is more than $$46\\,700$ but less than $$56\\,150?$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4960\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4920\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb189cdf-2b97-5db2-ae7a-d4991a5c036b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516843 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the annual income (in dollars) of a randomly selected employee. Then we have $X∼N(50\\,000,15\\,{000}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(46\\,700<X<56\\,150).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(46\\,700<X<56\\,150) & =P(\\frac{46\\,700-50\\,000}{15\\,000}<Z<\\frac{56\\,150-50\\,000}{15\\,000}) \\\\  & =P(-0.22<Z<0.41)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.22<Z<0.41) & =P(Z<0.41)-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.41)-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(0.41)-Φ(-0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.41) & =0.6591 \\\\ Φ(-0.22) & =0.4129.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.22<Z<0.41) & =Φ(0.41)-Φ(-0.22) \\\\  & =0.6591-0.4129 \\\\  & =0.2462 \\\\  & ≈0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected employee's income is more than $$46\\,700$ but less than $$56\\,150$ is approximately $0.25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516920 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516920 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "748cd377-8e7f-5346-aa3b-db4fe18ddcb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186516814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516922 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516922 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b705082-7666-5f62-ba6d-d0a2f1a92ff1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516922 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186493962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84ec6b96-1b9c-5175-9b67-1af8a664fc76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186302169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516957 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516957 